#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} {:answer/id #uuid "57f33323-a574-5d3a-9709-4eb09849a56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295774} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d54f7c-6d02-5810-afd3-16ba3cfaf37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296154} {:answer/id #uuid "bba10e7d-2cc2-5341-a2f3-b9fb123d6f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296250} {:answer/id #uuid "e870b774-8e7e-5746-ab82-10ba9e3d3195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} :answer-field/id #uuid "543e005e-a445-5e44-847a-061f1c3bcb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\n- Its range is $y>0$\n- It is an increasing function\n- $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}]}
